  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-09-30T09:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/4d19-400o400o1-VFK8xEhqzxtmTBMGXmAMUR.png",
   :session/title "Datomic Deep Dive",
   :session/description
   "You chose Datomic for production, and now you carry the weight of your past decisions. Or you know the basics of Datomic but don't yet feel confident taking it there. This advanced follow-up to the Amazing Day of Datomic is about the problems that only surface at scale: a schema migration that won't apply, a query that was fast in dev and times out in prod, latency that spikes every time a new index is adopted, and a partitioning schema you have to choose up front, before the data can tell you what's right. We'll work against a Datomic Pro database, troubleshooting problems together. You'll leave able to read what your system is telling you, with a clear sense of where to look when something is slow or wrong, and the confidence to make decisions.\n\n## João Nascimento Mello\n\nMello is a software engineer at Nubank who has worked across different parts of the company, helping teams find good solutions to hard problems and maybe liking them a little too much. He is also a dad of two, so most of his time outside work is already taken – and whatever is left usually goes to rest.",
   :session/external-id "1268217",
   :session/subtitle "João Nascimento Mello",
   :session/location :location.type/w201ab,
   :session/type :session.type/workshop,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT180M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-09-30T09:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/b5b1-400o400o1-X45Xxb3gi8KRSe9dyx2WyE.jpg",
   :session/title "Clojure Camp Coding Jam",
   :session/description
   "Write some code and learn from your peers. In this hands-on jam you'll team up - first in a mob session, then in a pairing session - to tackle a problem in Clojure, with prompts for both first-timers and seasoned hands. Bring a laptop and a willingness to think out loud; leave with new tools, tricks, and opinions from people who solve problems differently than you.\n\n## Rafal Dittwald\n\nRafal hasn't decided what he is yet, but he's spent the past several years: attempting to get multiple startups off the ground; mentoring hundreds of web development students; and writing many thousands of lines of code (mostly Clojure). Often, he encounters an innocuous question, like \"what is the best way to shuffle cards?\" and then can't help but spend an inordinate amount of time chasing the answers to these questions. Sometimes he gives talks about what he's found.",
   :session/external-id "1263356",
   :session/subtitle "Rafal Dittwald",
   :session/location :location.type/w201cd,
   :session/type :session.type/workshop,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT180M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-09-30T09:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/6d09-400o400o1-wWKvp78brYhxCLeqquXVb4.png",
   :session/title "Babashka: fast native Clojure scripting",
   :session/description
   "Babashka is a fast, native Clojure scripting runtime. It starts instantly and comes with batteries included, so you can write the scripts you'd normally write in bash. Keep writing the Clojure you already know.\r\n\r\nThis hands-on workshop covers the whole lifecycle of a babashka tool, from a quick script to a published, installable CLI app. We assume you know the basics of Clojure and won't explain the language itself.\r\n\r\nTopics include:\r\n\r\n- Setting up your dev environment\r\n- Managing projects with babashka tasks (bb.edn)\r\n- A tour of built-in libraries (fs, process, http-client, and more)\r\n- Writing and running tests\r\n- Building a CLI with subcommands and automatic help\r\n- Programming a terminal UI (TUI)\r\n- Producing a small web app\r\n- Publishing via GitHub or as an installable tool with bbin\r\n\r\nEvery concept comes with an exercise, building toward one culminating CLI app. There will be lots of interaction and fun!\n\n## Michiel Borkent\n\nMichiel Borkent, known online as @borkdude, is a passionate open source Clojure developer. He’s helping developers to write cleaner, faster, and more enjoyable code with popular tools like clj-kondo, babashka, SCI, nbb, and squint. When he’s not deep in parentheses, you’ll find him at the piano or enjoying a walk outdoors.\n\n## Rahul De\n\nI am primarily an SRE dedicated to making simpler tools to bridge the gap between Dev and Ops. As a die hard Clojure fan, I am fascinated to use its power and simplicity to make infra simple too; a place where it is rarely seen but can be of immense value. Mostly based in London, UK, I love to organise and attend meetups and conferences around increasing diversity in tech, functional programming and food.",
   :session/external-id "1255061",
   :session/subtitle "Michiel Borkent, Rahul De",
   :session/location :location.type/w205ab,
   :session/type :session.type/workshop,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT180M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-09-30T09:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/daa7-400o400o1-hPTWNfeCBqpULyFHKn4GzT.jpg",
   :session/title "Backseat Driver - Calva Tools for AI agents",
   :session/description
   "Learn how to power up your AI agent with Calva's Clojure tooling. Since Backseat Driver is a Calva extension, we will start by establishing how Calva supports REPL work and Clojure structural editing. Then we will go practical and hands-on, building some useful things. We will use a GitHub repository with all workshop materials, including slides, exercises, scripts, and some example prompts.\r\n\r\nExample topics:\r\n\r\n* Giving the agent access to Calva's REPL sessions\r\n* Native Copilot tools vs MCP (other harnesses)\r\n* The Backseat Driver toolbox: REPL, REPL log, structural editing\r\n* Calva's multi-user REPL, for parallelized agent work\r\n* Convincing the agent to use the tools\r\n* The Awesome Backseat Driver plugin marketplace\r\n\r\nPreparations. Arrive at the workshop with a working Calva development environment. You will also need an LLM subscription and an AI harness for VS Code, like Copilot, Cursor, ECA, etc. Preferably one that supports workspace-local MCP servers.\n\n## Peter Strömberg\n\nPeter Strömberg, a.k.a. PEZ, is by profession a frontend developer using primarily ClojureScript and Replicant. By night he turns into The Calva Guy, pondering how VS Code can better serve its users with Clojure tooling. With Joyride as the vehicle, he also tries to persuade VS Code users to adopt a more Emacs-like hacker attitude towards their editor. Peter is convinced that a developer environment without a Clojure REPL is a broken environment.",
   :session/external-id "1263446",
   :session/subtitle "Peter Strömberg",
   :session/location :location.type/w205cd,
   :session/type :session.type/workshop,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT180M"}
