  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-09-30T13:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/b6b1-400o400o1-GrY4UTo5VnBWhVuxER1xiu.png",
   :session/title "Fast, Lean, Native Clojure",
   :session/description
   "Working with native libraries and off-heap memory has historically been awkward and cumbersome. With the advent of tools like Project Panama and GraalVM's Native Image, Clojure developers now have powerful tools for working within and alongside native libraries written in c, c++, rust, and more. Using a combination of instruction and hands-on learning, this workshop will guide participants in the best practices of leveraging the native ecosystem.\r\n\r\nWe will learn how to improve startup time, lower CPU and memory usage, create standalone binaries, call native libraries, manipulate off-heap memory, reach new platforms, and create native libraries for embedding Clojure in non JVM applications.\n\n## Adrian Smith\n\nAdrian is a software engineer and the creator of Easel, a pure clojure IDE in the spirit of Emacs. He got started programming by making tic-tac-toe for his TI-83 calculator. Since then, he's built web, mobile, and desktop applications using python, javascript, ruby, php, objective-c, and c++ before finally finding Clojure. He believes that programming should be for everyone and that you shouldn't need a CS degree to make a simple web, desktop, or mobile app.",
   :session/external-id "1262039",
   :session/subtitle "Adrian Smith",
   :session/location :location.type/w201ab,
   :session/type :session.type/workshop,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT180M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-09-30T13:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/d15c-400o400o1-NrSvc221KczapFWeeBze5Z.jpg",
   :session/title "Clojure Camp Coding Jam - Part II!",
   :session/description
   "Write some code and learn from your peers. In this hands-on jam you'll team up - first in a mob session, then in a pairing session - to tackle a problem in Clojure, with prompts for both first-timers and seasoned hands. Bring a laptop and a willingness to think out loud; leave with new tools, tricks, and opinions from people who solve problems differently than you.\n\n## Jordan Miller\n\nJordan Miller\r\nStaff Engineer @Nubank, Co-Founder of Clojure Camp, Host/Producer of Lost in Lambduhhhs Podcast, Co-Author Clojure Brain Teasers, \r\n\r\nL. Jordan Miller (she/her/altgirl) is a Staff Software Engineer at Nubank where she focuses on Datomic developer success. Known in the streets for her passion for spreadsheets she enjoys communicating to engineers who appreciate the finest of functions. International globetrotter and stimulation seeker she has no idea how she manages to juggle so many projects; she is co-author of Clojure Brain Teasers, co-founder of Clojure Camp and producer/host of the Lost In Lambduhhs podcast. Check out her linktree: https://linktr.ee/lambduhhh",
   :session/external-id "1266604",
   :session/subtitle "Jordan Miller",
   :session/location :location.type/w201cd,
   :session/type :session.type/workshop,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT180M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-09-30T13:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/92cf-400o400o1-h5iwi1d9yNDVAazq1XvV4G.jpg",
   :session/title "AI Tools & Practices Showcase",
   :session/description
   "This fast-paced session of attendee demos will be a showcase of tools and practices that are making a real difference for Clojure developers today. You will see editor tools, REPL integration, connections to external systems, managing agents, and more! All experience levels are welcome. Expand the horizon of what you can do with AI and Clojure and bring those ideas back to your own projects.\r\nThe format will consist of a series of short demos (10 minutes each), culminating in a speaker panel Q&A with the audience. Due to the rapid pace of the AI development scene, demos will be proposed and chosen by the attendees a few weeks before the event.\n\n## Eric Dallo\n\nSoftware Engineer at Nubank, Emacs fanatic, NixOS enthusiast, and maintainer of some open-source projects like clojure-lsp, emacs-lsp, clj-easy and others, focusing on the developer experience.\r\n",
   :session/external-id "1261261",
   :session/subtitle "Eric Dallo",
   :session/location :location.type/w205abcd,
   :session/type :session.type/workshop,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT180M"}
  {:session/external-id "8a72337e-3194-44a0-8dff-5db087ebc2bb",
   :session/title "Meet and Mix",
   :session/time #time/zdt "2026-09-30T19:00-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/duration "PT180M",
   :session/location :location.type/_206ab,
   :session/type :session.type/break,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/description
   "Arrive during the first 30 minutes of the Meet and Mix and enjoy one complimentary drink (alcoholic or non-alcoholic).\n\nAfter that, there'll be a cash bar and a light buffet with snacks to accompany alcoholic drinks. Please note that this is an after-dinner activity, so come having already eaten.\n\nAt the Meet and Mix, networking becomes more than small talk: it’s a chance to make real connections.\n\nMeet new members of the community.\nConnect with well-known Clojure voices.\nExchange ideas, stories, and experiences.\nAnd turn those encounters into relationships that last long after the Conj.\n\nDon’t stay on the sidelines.\n\nCome mingle, find your people, and get closer to the heart of the Clojure community."}
