  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T09:05-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/e9c3-400o400o1-SwfF1EF31Tj7FxrWa76PBj.jpg",
   :session/title "In Context",
   :session/description
   "Building flexible systems means embracing the ever-changing shape of information as it flows throughout. How can we document and enforce our expectations without making things brittle? This talk examines some new features coming to Clojure that aim to address this challenge.\n\n## Rich Hickey\n\nRich Hickey, the author of Clojure and designer of Datomic, is a software developer with over 35 years of experience in various domains. ",
   :session/external-id "1299890",
   :session/subtitle "Rich Hickey",
   :session/location :location.type/w207abcd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T09:55-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/ad41-400o400o1-D76WXrPyZ26wNPu3RPmgxx.png",
   :session/title "Powering a 3D Game Engine with Clojure",
   :session/description
   "In this experience report, a 20-year veteran of C++ and C# game development recounts their experience building a real-time 3D game engine powered by Clojure: the motivation, surprising benefits, challenges, and lessons learned when using idiomatic Clojure for real-time 3D game development.\n\n## Jared Cone\n\nI've been making games for 20 years. I was the lead programmer and\r\nprincipal engineer for Rocket League, from prototype to production to 10 years as a live-service game. After a decade of admiring Clojure from afar I've finally taken the plunge by building a 3D game engine in Clojure to find out what the language can really do.",
   :session/external-id "1248150",
   :session/subtitle "Jared Cone",
   :session/location :location.type/w207abcd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T10:45-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/cf34-400o400o1-s8t2rR1yJvCzh9cKutdeWE.png",
   :session/title "Clojure in Academia",
   :session/description
   "What happens when you introduce Clojure and functional programming to students who aren’t looking for it?\r\nAt EPITA (Paris), we teach a course on functional programming using Clojure, focused on immutability, recursion, and lazy evaluation. Most students don’t fully “get it” by the end. A few do—and that difference is striking.\r\nThis talk is a candid look at that gap. Which ideas consistently fail to land? What clicks for the minority who connect with the model? How do constraints of a traditional curriculum shape the outcome?\r\nWe’ll also share the exercises and infrastructure we use to run the course at scale, and what they reveal about learning (and not learning) Clojure.\r\nAn honest account of teaching FP with Clojure: partial success, unexpected insights, and open questions.\n\n## Jim Newton\n\nJim 38 years as Lisp programmer, and assistant research professor, teaching mathematics and programming at an international, Paris based engineering school.",
   :session/external-id "1228103",
   :session/subtitle "Jim Newton",
   :session/location :location.type/w207abcd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T11:35-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/0b87-400o400o1-XVTHUwsqhUhusXd6sahL6p.png",
   :session/title "Would you believe it's jank?",
   :session/description
   "jank is the cutting-edge native Clojure dialect, built on LLVM, with seamless C++ interop. jank is unlocking rich interop with the native world, competitive performance, and an easy native embedding story.\r\n\r\nIn this talk, we'll cover how and where jank is innovating, the amazing things jank can do, and why having a rich native Clojure dialect matters. This will include topics like our unprecedented C++ interop, gorgeous error messages, impressive performance, easy binary distribution, and more.\r\n\r\nViewers will leave this talk inspired to use jank for game development, desktop GUI apps, scientific computing, and whatever they can dream.\n\n## Jeaye Wilkerson\n\nJeaye has a background in C++ systems programming, specifically with games and game engines. For the past two years, he's been focusing full-time on building jank so that the worlds of C++ and Clojure can be merged.",
   :session/external-id "1242600",
   :session/subtitle "Jeaye Wilkerson",
   :session/location :location.type/w207abcd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/external-id "16b6d30f-2d60-48d4-81d8-cd9a57aa57a8",
   :session/title "Lunch Meetup",
   :session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T12:15-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/duration "PT85M",
   :session/location :location.type/off-site,
   :session/type :session.type/break,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/description
   "Find your group in the Compass app, sign up, and head out for lunch together."}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T13:40-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/95d6-400o400o1-V89Bwoo1NkzDxF8RM4vwMm.png",
   :session/title "Porting Ideas, Not Code",
   :session/description
   "Clojure changed how many developers think about data: immutable facts, temporal structure over time, and code as data. But what changes when these ideas move beyond the Clojure ecosystem?\r\n\r\nStardust explores how Clojure and Datomic concepts can be reimagined in a language-agnostic data platform for messy, real-world open data. It looks at what transfers cleanly, what requires new trade offs outside the JVM, and how real-world measurement reshapes system design. It’s not about preserving a stack, but about carrying forward ideas like simplicity, compatibility, and care for data across different languages and systems.\n\n## Delaney Gillilan\n\nDelaney has a rich and varied background from full stack game development, military systems of record, gambling and slot machines, to even a stint in the circus. He has a love for creating tools and engines for other creators to use and moves fast! ",
   :session/external-id "1262514",
   :session/subtitle "Delaney Gillilan",
   :session/location :location.type/w207ab,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T13:40-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/6d2f-400o400o1-Y2ttK8PxRpNQvvwLoH4jni.jpg",
   :session/title
   "Reimagining the Spreadsheet with ClojureScript and SCI",
   :session/description
   "What if you could have a spreadsheet that combines:\r\n- the layout, styling and interactivity of a web page, \r\n- the reactivity of a spreadsheet, \r\n- and the expressiveness of Clojure? \r\n\r\nHow Clojure enables the next generation of spreadsheets.\n\n## Markus Holmström\n\nHaving worked many years at an investment bank in Zurich, I have seen firsthand the power spreadsheets give users and also their limits. \r\nMy current focus is to improve the powers and to remove the limits of the spreadsheet.",
   :session/external-id "1263871",
   :session/subtitle "Markus Holmström",
   :session/location :location.type/w207cd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T14:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/6d65-400o400o1-BaPFdgGS1o6jBjCgbX7tLm.jpg",
   :session/title "The Game That Deleted Its Frontend",
   :session/description
   "I built a multiplayer board-game table as a ClojureScript re-frame SPA. Thousands of games got played on it, and the client-side state handling was the part I never stopped fighting. So now my apps have no frontend code at all: the server owns every bit of state through a core.async.flow CQRS backbone, and streams the UI over a single SSE connection to Datastar.\r\n\r\nThe main idea you'll take home is routing data by what it needs: user actions go into an append-only event log (history, replay and undo come for free), live cursors go over a lossy broadcast that never touches the database.\r\n\n\n## Fabian Merkle\n\nI'm Fabian Merkle, a Clojure consultant in Dubai, six years full-time in Clojure and twelve as a developer. \r\n\r\nI work with early-stage startups across the US and Europe — fintech, gaming, ERP, EdTech. ",
   :session/external-id "1263920",
   :session/subtitle "Fabian Merkle",
   :session/location :location.type/w207ab,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T14:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/3093-400o400o1-Rk9yGoJxzgxDnAGnnWDcXk.jpg",
   :session/title
   "From OutOfMemoryError to Root Cause: Analyzing Clojure Heap Dumps",
   :session/description
   "When a Clojure process runs out of memory, the JVM could give you a heap dump, but most Clojure developers have never opened one. This talk walks through my diagnostic process for finding memory leaks using Eclipse MAT, including how to map Java class names back to Clojure constructs and how to recognize the patterns that trip us up (memoized functions, go-loops capturing dynamic vars, unbounded registries growing during long-running REPL sessions). You will leave with a repeatable workflow for taking, opening, and analyzing heap dumps from any Clojure process. The talk concludes with an investigation whose fixes reduced memory usage of an internal test suite from up to 50GB to a couple of GB, plus a memory issue with clojure.core.cache that would be unfeasible to find without this heap dump analysis.\n\n## Santiago Cabrera\n\nLead Software Engineer at Nubank, where I've worked since June 2022 on the multi-service test infrastructure used across Nubank's Clojure services, a domain where memory profiling and REPL-driven development are daily concerns. I run Nubank's Engineering Excellence Tech Forum and recently spoke about Clojure at the BogotaJVM meetup, hosted at Oracle's offices for Java's 30th anniversary.",
   :session/external-id "1232577",
   :session/subtitle "Santiago Cabrera",
   :session/location :location.type/w207cd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
