  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T15:40-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/3470-400o400o1-9aYY2c4D5CvgyoF4dUuDyP.jpg",
   :session/title "Simplicity Ain't Free",
   :session/description
   "We've all watched \"Simplicity Ain't Easy\" and \"Simple Made Easy\" enough times to do the call-and-response, dictionary-definition slides and all. Personally, I've been deeply shaped by the ideas from Stu, Rich and others in the community. I love simplicity.\r\n\r\nAnd, while simplicity is one of Clojure's great gifts to software, it's not free. Finding the \"simples\" in a domain takes time, and composing them brings real costs. Sometimes an easy or expedient implementation — the complected, messy thing that works today — is what keeps our product or company alive long enough to improve tomorrow.\r\n\r\nDrawing on these evergreen talks and Richard Gabriel's \"Worse is Better\" essays, this talk asks: when should we decomplect, when should we choose ease, and is that choice even binary? We'll build a set of clues, considerations, and guiding principles to help us read trade-offs and context so we can deliver what we need today without cutting ourselves off from the path to Better.\n\n## Aaron Brooks\n\nAaron has been writing software for over 40 years and Clojure since its early days. He was part of the community before the first Clojure/conj, has given talks at Clojure conferences, and ran a Clojure meetup for more than a decade. He spent over 10 years leading Clojure training, adoption, consulting, and engineering at Viasat, worked at a Clojure startup, and now leads engineering operations at Supabase. The ideas in talks like \"Simple Made Easy\" and \"Simplicity Ain't Easy\" reshaped how he thinks about engineering — which is exactly why he wants to look hard at what simplicity costs, and how to apply it well.",
   :session/external-id "1263325",
   :session/subtitle "Aaron Brooks",
   :session/location :location.type/w207ab,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T15:40-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/270d-400o400o1-wWuemtwSj9VVthYMijwBFJ.jpg",
   :session/title
   "Live Systems, Live Thinking: Materializing Production Traces into AI-Assisted REPL Flows",
   :session/description
   "REPL-driven development is often treated as a local expression-evaluation mechanism. But when production incidents span complex business flows, we. abandon our flow state for slow, diagnostics. What if your architecture could automatically materialize individual production incident profiles directly into your local REPL?\r\n\r\nThis talk explores an advanced debugging workflow that scales conversational programming across multi-service business states. We explore the architecture of an automated pipeline that queries a live, failing entity's history, attributes, and generates a local scratch namespace file populated with real parameters, commands, and events. Using state-flow monadic testing and local variable-capture macros, developers can interactively step through historical transitions and hot-patch code. Finally, we show how this single-entity precision unlocks a massive future: using AI assistants to safely execute and scale these REPL flows across dozens of edge cases simultaneously.\n\n## Juliana Prieto\n\nJuliana Prieto is a software engineer at Nu, working with Clojure on backend financial systems. She enjoys learning deeply, teaching others, and communicating technical ideas clearly. While she has a strong interest in frontend, product, and user experience, her current work is focused on backend systems, debugging workflows, and real-world business flows. She is especially curious about software architecture, feedback loops, and the way engineering practices shape how teams understand, change, and trust complex systems.",
   :session/external-id "1263970",
   :session/subtitle "Juliana Prieto",
   :session/location :location.type/w207cd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T16:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/f005-400o400o1-YEPz56HyV9pVjgrFdvh6pW.jpg",
   :session/title
   "Building Simple Systems in the World of Natural Language Programming",
   :session/description
   "Apps are getting more complex, MVP scope is growing, and developers are being asked to build more than ever faster than ever. New information systems are also expected to be compatible with agentic features rather than creating friction. Most importantly, developers are required to be accountable for their work despite producing more code than ever. \r\n\r\nHow do you build reasonable systems with AI when the default mode of working with Agentic Coding leads to architectural drift and slop? Is there a way to bottom out AI decision making at a structural level? Can Markdown files ever be enough?\r\n\r\nIn this talk, I show you how to use ideas from software engineering, a small set of foundational rules, composable building blocks, and an immutable single source of truth to constrain AI agents and produce reliable behavior, based on our experience in production.\r\n\r\nYou'll come away from the talk understanding how to create successful software in the post-AI world.\r\n\n\n## Cameron Barre\n\nCameron Barre is a long time Clojure professional currently solving problems in the realm of AI assisted coding and building systems that integrate AI features as CTO of ObneyAI",
   :session/external-id "1250010",
   :session/subtitle "Cameron Barre",
   :session/location :location.type/w207ab,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-01T16:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/8ee6-400o400o1-7tBMwwv5xBvmAoE9v54Lx2.png",
   :session/title "What tipped the scales for Clojure",
   :session/description
   "For 15 years, Clojure's headwinds (smaller hiring pool, thinner library long tail, the inertia of moving off an incumbent stack) outweighed its ergonomic benefits (REPL-driven iteration, immutable data, image introspection, succinct DSLs) for most teams — the right call while humans typed. Agentic coding changes both sides of the scale at once: the AI absorbs most of the headwinds (the surface tax is paid by something that doesn't tire), while Clojure's ergonomics multiply a developer that iterates orders of magnitude faster than a human can. After starting a production AI product on TypeScript and Python (~90,000 lines) and rewriting it in ~12,000 lines of Clojure and ClojureScript with an AI agent as the primary pair, the rewrite paid for itself in months and has compounded gains daily ever since. You'll leave with a framework for re-running that calculation in your own setup — and a usable answer: don't let the agent dictate your language. The agent can handle yours.\n\n## Brendan Foote\n\nBrendan Foote co-founded JoinIn.ai, where a two-engineer team rewrote a production real-time AI meeting product from 90,000 lines of TypeScript and Python into 12,000 lines of Clojure and ClojureScript — the case study of this talk. He has been working in Clojure since 2013, when he read *The Joy of Clojure* and gave a \"Why Clojure?\" presentation in his Master's in Software Engineering program. He has spoken on Clojure at MSSE Barcamp events, including a talk on a production Clojure tool from a prior financial-services role — 87 lines of code that parsed 750MB of nightly XML and trivially parallelized the work. With 20+ years of engineering experience, he has been making the case for Clojure for over a decade, and is making it again — now as an applied lesson from rebuilding a production AI product with an agent as the primary pair.",
   :session/external-id "1240615",
   :session/subtitle "Brendan Foote",
   :session/location :location.type/w207cd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
