  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-02T09:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/082f-400o400o1-Qjyr1C17HnZKb5eRTdy5r.jpg",
   :session/title "Making Simple Faster",
   :session/description
   "Clojure succeeded by delivering a profoundly simple computing model that changed how we manage state and complexity. Yet, over the last fifteen years, both our real-world production experience and the underlying JVM have evolved dramatically. This talk explores how we can leverage modern JVM capabilities to advance Clojure's core abstractions without compromising its core philosophy.\r\n\r\nWe share concrete research improvements to core language features, including a provably better protocol implementation and a novel compiler approach to vars that yields major startup performance gains. From this foundation, we make a call for deeper research into a modernized compiler and runtime.\n\n## Chris Nuernberger\n\n\"Chris attended the University of Colorado at Boulder earning a BS in computer science. Since then, his technical excellence has driven the development of many industry-leading products. Startups love him: with Anark, he brought advanced computer graphics and designer-developer interactions to the web. At SRC he deployed new lossless compression algorithms for GIS systems. At NVIDIA, he led an international team to develop the powerful and popular PhysX debugger. And, in recent years he has focused on applying neural networks and other data-intensive techniques to stubborn business problems, leaving a trail of happy customers in his wake. He has the distinction of both releasing software on CD-ROM, and managing cloud-native machine learning systems with high throughput and availability. Outside of work Chris enjoys his family, rock climbing, and pushing computers to perform beyond their limits.\"",
   :session/external-id "1262755",
   :session/subtitle "Chris Nuernberger",
   :session/location :location.type/w207ab,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-02T09:30-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/d72b-400o400o1-Bg8dBZFeQFcgb4Yy952kPN.jpg",
   :session/title "Production ClojureDart for iOS, Android, and macOS",
   :session/description
   "Over the past twelve months a tiny team has used ClojureDart (Clojure compiled to Dart, running on Flutter) to build more than a dozen production apps for iOS, Android, and macOS, most deployed internally and several bound for the app stores. The apps include a document scanner with on-device OpenCV, a transit tracker with realtime GTFS, a venture-capital deck triager with on-device LLM evaluation, a notes app with on-device sentence embeddings, a marketplace, an encyclopedia, a financial charting app, and several more. This experience report distills the patterns that survived the trip from prototype to production: a single-atom-plus-`:watch` architecture that replaces Riverpod and BLoC, a curated catalog of cljd-flutter pitfalls (each one a lost afternoon you don't have to spend), and a reusable scaffold for new apps. Attendees will leave with a grounded view of where ClojureDart is strong, where it still has sharp edges, and a checklist of decisions to make on day one.\n\n## Burin Choomnuan\n\nWith over two decades of experience in software development, Burin Choomnuan has evolved alongside the technology landscape, moving from Java to Ruby to Clojure, then on to ClojureScript, and most recently to ClojureDart for mobile and desktop work. Over the past year he has shipped a fleet of production ClojureDart apps for iOS, Android, and macOS, alongside Clojure backends and ClojureScript single-page apps that share a single mental model end-to-end.\r\n\r\nHis current interests are the convergence of functional programming with AI-assisted development, building maintainable agentic workflows in Clojure, and sharing the patterns and pitfalls a tiny team learns by shipping at pace. When he isn't coding, he's writing experience reports for fellow developers who appreciate the art of elegant, productive engineering.",
   :session/external-id "1257073",
   :session/subtitle "Burin Choomnuan",
   :session/location :location.type/w207cd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-02T10:20-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/585e-400o400o1-HNcFDQw5asBdT3FPqFNq4t.jpg",
   :session/title "Compiling Clojure in Parallel using Bazel",
   :session/description
   "Bazel is a fast and correct multi-language, monorepo build tool built by Google. We built https://github.com/griffinbank/rules_clojure to add Clojure support to Bazel, granting superpowers such as parallel compilation, parallel test execution and test caching. At Griffin, we reduced uberjar times from minutes to seconds, and CI times from hours to minutes. \r\n\r\nIn this talk we'll talk about the advantages and tradeoffs of using Bazel, the JVM classloader and Clojure compilation model, and the challenges of building parallel compilation of clojure sources. \n\n## Allen Rohner\n\nAllen has been using Clojure since the pre-1.0 days and has contributed to dozens of Clojure libraries. He has founded two venture-backed Clojure startups, CircleCI and Griffin Financial Technology. ",
   :session/external-id "1261206",
   :session/subtitle "Allen Rohner",
   :session/location :location.type/w207ab,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-02T10:20-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/1a4e-400o400o1-EXCnYDKjxzpM34sT6j9Lpq.jpg",
   :session/title "Where Did This div Come From?",
   :session/description
   "Fourteen years ago, Bret Victor argued that creators need an immediate connection to what they make. Clojure already lived it: the REPL, then Figwheel and shadow-cljs in the browser. But it stops at the rendered page: you can change the code and watch the page update, yet you can't point at something on it and ask which Clojure form made it. Render Hiccup to HTML and the div forgets it was ever Clojure code on a line. Three clicks deep? Grep and guesswork.\r\n\r\nThis talk builds the other half: a source inspector wiring your app, browser, and editor into one loop. Hover an element and the editor jumps to the Hiccup that made it. Put your cursor on a view and the element lights up, definition told apart from call site.\r\n\r\nIt's surprisingly little code. We build it live, end to end, so you can wire the same loop into your own server-rendered Hiccup app.\n\n## Patrick de Kruif\n\nPatrick de Kruif has built software in Clojure professionally for the past five years, and tinkered with it longer still as a side interest before that. His work is mostly web applications backed by databases, and pretty much everything around them. From schema and UI through to CI, deployment, and the devops that keeps it all running. He's happiest working on interactive systems, with a weakness for small, sharp tools over large frameworks.",
   :session/external-id "1263299",
   :session/subtitle "Patrick de Kruif",
   :session/location :location.type/w207cd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-02T11:10-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/187b-400o400o1-EQPUNAGrxDs1knyjWpMhkP.jpg",
   :session/title "The Code Was the Easy Part",
   :session/description
   "When I decided to contribute to Clojure core, the code felt like the obvious challenge. It isn't. Before writing a single line, there is a harder problem: understanding what is actually wrong, why the current behavior exists, what possibilities have already been considered or currently exist, and what reasons quietly rule most of them out. This talk is about what I learned from that process, through real contributions to Clojure core (some merged, some not): the code was hard, but that was the part I was ready for.\n\n## Arthur Fücher\n\nArthur Fücher is a programmer who loves meeting new people and having great conversations. He's the proud father of Laura and a passionate Magic: The Gathering player.\r\nArthur is the organizer and host of the Clojure BR community, where he works to grow the Clojure ecosystem by sharing content in Portuguese. He's also a member of the InnerSource Commons Foundation.\r\nHe has been working with Clojure for the past six years at Nubank, across a variety of business domains, and is currently focused on improving Developer Experience.",
   :session/external-id "1263835",
   :session/subtitle "Arthur Fücher",
   :session/location :location.type/w207ab,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
  {:session/time #time/zdt "2026-10-02T11:10-04:00[America/New_York]",
   :session/image
   "https://cdn.sessionize.com/image/c64c-400o400o1-7RDfwYeY8fEmJMdmRRfiTB.jpg",
   :session/title "Applied Logic Programming",
   :session/description
   "\r\nAt Gateless we’ve been shipping mortgage underwriting automation with Clara Rules since 2021. This talk is about the lessons we’ve learning using Logic Programming in production, at scale, for over five years. This talk will cover a very brief introduction to rules development, but focus more on a case study in how we leveraged the paradigm and broken past roadblocks with advanced tooling. At one point every production release broke something, and no amount of testing would surface the answers. How would you ensure quality in a high stakes system with thousands of facts and rules?\r\n\r\nWe’ll give a brief introduction to the business problems we’ve been solving and make the case for Logic Programming. We’ll review our architecture as it pertains to the use of rules. Then we’ll dive deeper into our tooling, and how we use it to ensure quality. \n\n## Daniel Glauser\n\nDaniel has been working with Clojure for over fifteen years, and software for almost thirty years. Throughout his career he’s worked at large companies like NBC-Universal and Comcast, as well as a host of smaller startups. He’s currently a VP of Engineering at Gateless, a mortgage software company focused on automating underwriting of residential home loans. When he’s not working Daniel enjoys camping, photography, and videography.\n\n## Jose Gomez\n\nJose Gomez has been working in software for approximately fifteen years, and with Clojure for over ten years. Throughout his career he's worked at companies including Bristol Myers Squibb, Express Scripts, and Guaranteed Rate, as well as smaller startups and boutique consulting firms. He's currently a Senior Principal Software Engineer at Gateless, a mortgage software company focused on automating underwriting of residential home loans. Jose focuses on distributed systems and product engineering, and has contributed to open source work including Clara Rules and Futurama. When he's not working Jose enjoys building computers, playing online MMOs, and listening to an ever-growing audiobook collection.",
   :session/external-id "1259441",
   :session/subtitle "Daniel Glauser, Jose Gomez",
   :session/location :location.type/w207cd,
   :session/type :session.type/session,
   :session/capacity 500,
   :session/duration "PT40M"}
